      "title": "King Croc",
      "text": "<h3>King Croc</h3>\n<p class=\"sub\"><i>Crocodiles have existed for more than 200 million years, yet they are obviously not primitive. Karrens P finds out the secret of how they survive as the king reptile.</i></p>\n<p><b>A</b> Crocodiles have gone through difficult times in history and witnessed the disappearance of huge species and the emergence of new ones. Dinosaurs disappeared about 65 million years ago, yet today crocodile populations are growing, and some types are no longer endangered. Thanks to changes in their body shape and their ability to adapt to different climates, they survived when most reptiles of their period became extinct. During their time on Earth, they have endured the impacts of meteor strikes, planetary cooling, extreme shifts in the Earth’s tectonic surface, and profound climate changes. They were present for both the rise and the fall of the dinosaurs. This long survival record shows that crocodiles have not only adapted to countless environmental pressures but also outlived creatures that once dominated the Earth. Fossil records indicate their remarkable resilience, demonstrating a pattern of survival where other reptiles failed, proving that crocodiles are among the most successful survivors in evolutionary history.</p>\n<p><b>B</b> The first crocodile-like ancestors appeared about 230 million years ago, already possessing many of the features that make crocodiles such successful stealth hunters: a streamlined body, a long tail, protective armour, and long jaws. They have long heads and tails that help them change direction easily while moving through water. Over millions of years, these characteristics have remained largely unchanged because they work so well in helping crocodiles catch prey and avoid predators. The protective armour shields them from attacks, while their powerful tails and muscular limbs allow swift movement both in water and on land. Even the positioning of their eyes and nostrils on the top of their heads enables them to see and breathe while most of their bodies remain hidden underwater, making them expert ambush predators.</p>\n<p><b>C</b> Crocodiles possess a special heat-regulation system that allows them to maintain their body temperature, enabling them to function effectively in a variety of environmental conditions. This system involves both behavioural and physiological strategies. For instance, they may bask in the sun to warm up after a cool night, or retreat to shaded areas or water to avoid overheating during the hottest part of the day. Unlike mammals, which generate most of their heat internally, crocodiles rely heavily on external sources to regulate their body temperature, yet their adaptations are so effective that they can live in a wide range of climates. These methods ensure they can remain active for hunting or territorial defence without suffering from extreme temperature stress.</p>\n<p><b>D</b> Crocodiles can eat almost anything — plant or animal. If the environment changes or they are forced to move to a new area, they can consume whatever food is available. They can also survive for extremely long periods, up to a year, without eating. This dietary flexibility is one of their greatest survival tools, allowing them to adapt quickly when prey becomes scarce. In times of abundance, they may feast on fish, birds, or small mammals, while in harder times they can survive on carrion or even plant material. Such adaptability ensures they can maintain their strength and health even when their habitat undergoes significant changes, such as seasonal flooding or drought.</p>\n<p><b>E</b> Crocodiles face drought every year. During the summer, they retreat into dry caverns, remain still with their heads lowered, and stop eating. This process is known as aestivation. It is a survival strategy that helps them conserve energy and avoid harsh environmental conditions when water is unavailable. By entering this dormant state, crocodiles can endure periods when food and water sources are severely limited. Similar to hibernation in mammals, aestivation allows them to “wait out” unfavourable seasons until conditions improve, ensuring their survival through the toughest months of the year.</p>\n<p><b>F</b> Six years ago, Kennetts and Christian performed an extensive project examining the aestivation patterns of Australian freshwater crocodiles. One set of these animals inhabits an area that experiences a dry season lasting up to four months each year. Over this interval, the crocodiles withdraw from the creeks, settle in underground refuges, and no loss of body mass was recorded. In contrast, another population living by the Mackline River benefits from uninterrupted access to water. Every summer, the crocodiles from the arid region make their way from the waterways into sheltered caves.</p>\n<p><b>G</b> During aestivation, crocodiles show stable levels of bodily waste, indicating that their metabolism remains steady. Even after enduring this state for months, they exhibit no evidence of dehydration or other harmful health effects. This extended capacity to survive challenging conditions does not affect their growth rate or general physical state. Such durability is a crucial factor in explaining how crocodiles have persisted through millions of years of environmental change.</p>",
